Good progress! Down to 1 failure. The "Toggle-all updates when individual todos are checked" scenario fails because the toggle-all checkbox isn't checked after all individual toggles have been checked.

Let me look at the scenario:
```
  Scenario: Toggle-all updates when individual todos are checked
    Given I have todos: "Buy milk", "Buy eggs", "Pay bills"
    When I complete all todos individually
    Then the "toggle-all" checkbox should be checked
```

The `When I complete all todos individually` step clicks each toggle. But my app's toggle click handler listens for a `change` event (via `list.addEventListener('change', ...)`). Let me check if `.click()` on a checkbox fires the `change` event...

In happy-dom, `.click()` on a checkbox should toggle the `checked` property and fire a `change` event. Let me check my event handler:

```js
list.addEventListener('change', function (e) {
  if (e.target.classList.contains('toggle')) {
    var li = e.target.closest('li');
    if (li) toggleTodo(li.getAttribute('data-id'));
  }
});
```

And the `I complete all todos individually` step:
```js
When('I complete all todos individually', function () {
  const toggles = this.document.querySelectorAll('.todo-list .toggle');
  for (const t of toggles) { if (!t.checked) t.click(); }
});
```

The issue might be that when we click the toggle, it dispatches a change event which calls `toggleTodo`, which updates localStorage and re-renders. After re-render, the remaining toggles might have been replaced. Let me look at the step again - it queries `querySelectorAll` first and then iterates. But after re-render, the references to `t` may be stale.

Actually, wait - the step already checks `if (!t.checked) t.click()`. So it should work. But if the change event handler calls `render()` which replaces the DOM, then the second iteration might have issues with the stale node list.

Actually, `querySelectorAll` returns a static NodeList. So after the first click triggers a re-render, the second click on the old `t` reference might not be on the current DOM tree. But the click should still fire on the old element, and since the old element has been removed from the DOM, clicking it won't do anything.

This is a race condition / stale reference issue. Let me fix the step to re-query after each click:

edits.oldText:
When('I complete all todos individually', function () {
  const toggles = this.document.querySelectorAll('.todo-list .toggle');
  for (const t of toggles) { if (!t.checked) t.click(); }
});

edits.newText:
When('I complete all todos individually', function () {
  // Re-query after each click because re-render replaces DOM elements.
  let toggle = this.document.querySelector('.todo-list .toggle:not(:checked)');
  while (toggle) {
    toggle.click();
    toggle = this.document.querySelector('.todo-list .toggle:not(:checked)');
  }
});

